---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Documentation Templates - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="documentation" subChapterId="templates">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Templates remove the friction that stops people from writing documentation.
				They also provide a structure for the AI to follow when writing documentation drafts.
			</p>

			<p>
				The templates below follow industry standards where they exist (like ADRs)
				and practical patterns we've found effective for AI-assisted teams. Customize these to your team's needs.
			</p>

			<p>
				Consistency is what makes a folder of docs readable by tooling. On top of the
				per-type structures below, it helps to give every document the same small header.
				Google's{" "}
				<a href="https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/knowledge-catalog/blob/main/okf/SPEC.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">Open Knowledge Format (OKF)</a>{" "}
				is a minimal, tool-agnostic convention for exactly this: a directory of markdown
				files, each opening with a short YAML frontmatter block (the one required field is{" "}
				<code>type</code>). Any OKF-aware tool can then read your docs without repo-specific
				knowledge. The{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first chapter</a>{" "}
				covers the frontmatter fields in detail.
			</p>

			<h3>The Shared Header (Frontmatter)</h3>

			<p>
				Every concept doc opens with the same block. The AI reads it to decide whether a
				file is relevant before opening the whole thing, so keep the <code>description</code>{" "}
				to a single useful sentence.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Frontmatter
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
type: decision            # required. Pick a small, fixed vocabulary
title: Stateless BFF sessions
description: One sentence. This is what a folder index quotes.
status: accepted          # values depend on type (see below)
timestamp: 2026-05-28     # ISO date of the last meaningful change
owner: your-handle
tags: [auth, sessions]
---`}</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				<code>type</code> is free-text in OKF, but agree on a small closed set and stick to
				it: <code>decision</code>, <code>feature</code>, <code>guide</code>,{" "}
				<code>workflow</code>, <code>tech</code>, <code>external</code>. The <code>status</code>{" "}
				vocabulary depends on the type:
			</p>

			<div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
				<table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-stone-300">
							<th class="text-left py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Type</th>
							<th class="text-left py-2 font-semibold text-stone-800">Status values</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="text-stone-600">
						<tr class="border-b border-stone-200">
							<td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-700">decision</td>
							<td class="py-2">proposed, accepted, superseded, deprecated</td>
						</tr>
						<tr class="border-b border-stone-200">
							<td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-700">feature</td>
							<td class="py-2">draft, proposed, approved, in-progress, shipped, deprecated</td>
						</tr>
						<tr class="border-b border-stone-200">
							<td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-700">guide, tech, workflow</td>
							<td class="py-2">draft, active, deprecated</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<p>
				Two more conventions keep a bundle navigable. For <strong>naming</strong>, numbered
				types (decisions, features, workflows) use a zero-padded prefix like{" "}
				<code>0001-stateless-bff-sessions.md</code>; slug types (tech, guides, external) use
				kebab-case. For <strong>cross-links</strong>, link related docs by path rather than
				by name, for example <code>[ADR-0004](/decisions/0004-api-versioning.md)</code>, and
				put those same paths in relationship fields like <code>decisions:</code> on a feature.
			</p>

			<h3>Architecture Decision Record (ADR)</h3>

			<p>
				ADRs are a well-established format for recording technical decisions. The key
				sections are what was decided, why, and what alternatives were considered.
				These are short documents, typically under a page. For more on the ADR format,
				see <a href="https://adr.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">adr.github.io</a>.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					ADR Template
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
type: decision
title: <decision in a short noun phrase>
description: <one sentence: the rule that resulted>
status: proposed        # proposed | accepted | superseded | deprecated
timestamp: 2026-05-28
owner: your-handle
tags: []
---

# <Title>

## Context
What forces are at play? What problem or constraint prompted this?

## Decision
What we decided, stated plainly.

## Consequences
What becomes easier, harder, or newly required. Trade-offs accepted.

## Alternatives considered
- <Option>: why not.`}</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				Here is the same template filled in on a real project. Notice how much of the value
				is in the "why", the constraints, and the options that were rejected. That is the
				context an AI cannot reconstruct from the code.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Filled example: ADR-0001
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
type: decision
title: Stateless, cookie-based BFF sessions
description: BFF sessions are stateless, signed cookies (iron-session), no server-side store.
status: accepted
timestamp: 2026-05-28
owner: mvalstar
tags: [auth, bff, sessions]
---

# Stateless, cookie-based BFF sessions

## Context
Each Next.js app acts as a BFF in front of the shared Spring Boot API. We
need session handling on the BFF, but want to avoid running a shared session
store (Redis) for an early-phase, two-frontend system.

## Decision
Sessions are stateless: the BFF stores signed/encrypted session state in a
cookie (iron-session) rather than a server-side store. The session cookie is
SameSite=Strict; the auth-callback handoff uses a short-lived SameSite=Lax
cookie that is immediately swapped for the Strict session cookie.

## Consequences
- No Redis / shared store to run, scale, or secure.
- Horizontal scaling is trivial: any BFF instance can serve any request.
- Cookie size budget must be respected; large session payloads are out.
- CSRF strategy for cookie-based sessions remains an open question.

## Alternatives considered
- Server-side sessions (Redis): more infra to run; unnecessary at this scale.
- better-auth: pulls in more than a pure session-token use case needs.`}</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Feature Approach Document</h3>

			<p>
				Feature docs describe how a feature works and are the source of truth for what you
				are building. They are useful for onboarding (both humans and AI) and for agreeing on
				the design before implementation starts. Link the ADRs that constrain the feature so
				the AI reads them together.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Feature Doc Template
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
type: feature
title: <feature name>
description: <one sentence: what this feature is>
status: draft           # draft | proposed | approved | in-progress | shipped
timestamp: 2026-05-28
owner: your-handle
tags: []
decisions: []           # related ADR paths, e.g. [/decisions/0001-sessions.md]
---

# <Feature name>

## Summary
One paragraph: what this feature is and who it's for.

## Goals / non-goals
- Goals: ...
- Non-goals: ...

## Behavior
The source-of-truth description of how it works, user-facing and system
behavior. Specific enough that backend and frontend can build from it.

## API surface
Endpoints, request/response shapes, and error cases.

## Data
Tables, migrations, and key fields.

## Open questions
- ...`}</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Runbook</h3>

			<p>
				Runbooks are step-by-step guides for operational tasks. They're written for the
				person (or AI) who needs to do something right now, possibly under pressure.
				Keep them direct and action-oriented. In a docs bundle these are <code>type: guide</code>.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Runbook Template
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
type: guide
title: <task or scenario>
description: <one sentence: the situation this addresses>
status: active          # draft | active | deprecated
timestamp: 2026-05-28
owner: your-handle
tags: []
---

# Runbook: <Task or Scenario>

## When to use
What situation triggers this runbook.

## Prerequisites
Access, tools, or permissions needed.

## Steps
Numbered, specific steps. Include exact commands where applicable.

## Verification
How to confirm the task completed successfully.

## Troubleshooting
Common failures and how to resolve them.

## Escalation
Who to contact if this doesn't work.`}</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Workflow</h3>

			<p>
				Workflow docs model a domain process: the states something moves through and the
				transitions between them. They give the AI (and reviewers) a single place to check
				before touching state-machine logic. A state diagram, for example in Mermaid, reads
				well alongside the tables.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Workflow Template
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
type: workflow
title: <workflow name>
description: <one sentence: the process this models>
status: active          # draft | active | deprecated
timestamp: 2026-05-28
owner: your-handle
tags: []
features: []            # related feature paths
---

# <Workflow name>

## Purpose
What process this models and when it runs.

## States
| State | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| ...   | ...     |

## Transitions
For each transition: trigger, actor, guard conditions, and side effects
(events emitted, notifications sent).`}</div>
			</div>

			<h3>API Endpoint Documentation</h3>

			<p>
				API contracts often live inside a feature doc's "API surface" section, and many
				teams generate them from code annotations instead of writing them by hand. When you
				do keep a standalone reference, this structure works:
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					API Doc Template
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`# <Endpoint Name>
Method: GET | POST | PUT | DELETE
Path:   /api/v1/...
Auth:   Required | Public

## Description
What this endpoint does and when to use it.

## Request
Headers, query params, and body shape with types.

## Response
Success and error response shapes with status codes.

## Error Cases
Known error scenarios and what causes them.`}</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Test Plan</h3>

			<p>
				Test plans describe what to test and how, so the AI generates meaningful tests
				rather than boilerplate. They can stand alone or live next to the feature doc.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Test Plan Template
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`# Test Plan: <Feature Name>

## Scope
What's being tested and what's out of scope.

## Test Scenarios
The key user flows and edge cases to cover, grouped by area.

## Environment
Setup requirements, test data, and dependencies.

## Acceptance Criteria
What "passing" looks like for this feature.`}</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Record Your Conventions</h3>

			<p>
				The templates only stay consistent if the rules behind them are written down. Keep a
				short conventions guide in your docs folder that defines your frontmatter fields,
				your <code>type</code> and <code>status</code> vocabularies, and your naming and
				cross-link rules. Then record the choice of format itself as an ADR, so the next
				person, and the next AI session, knows the docs follow a standard and why. A{" "}
				<code>_template.md</code> in each folder that people copy is the cheapest way to keep
				new docs in shape.
			</p>

			<h3>Turning Templates into Skills</h3>

			<p>
				A template sitting in a docs folder is useful. A template encoded as an AI skill
				is more useful because it gets applied automatically. When you build a skill
				for a document type, the AI produces a properly structured document every time
				someone triggers it, with no need to look up or copy the template.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Example: ADR skill (SKILL.md)
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`---
name: adr-writer
description: Create Architecture Decision Records. Use when the user
  asks to document a technical decision, create an ADR, or record why
  a specific approach was chosen.
---

# ADR Writer

Write an Architecture Decision Record by copying the template in
docs/decisions/_template.md. Before writing, ask clarifying questions
to understand the decision context if it's not already clear.

## Steps

1. Check docs/decisions/ for the latest ADR number and increment it
2. Ask the user about the context, alternatives considered, and
   reasoning if not already provided
3. Fill in the frontmatter and body from the template
4. Save as docs/decisions/NNNN-[slug].md
5. Add a row to docs/decisions/index.md`}</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				See <a href="/requirements-writing/skills">Skills</a> for a walkthrough of
				how to build and manage skills for your team.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Don't over-engineer your templates. A template with 20 sections will feel
					like a chore to fill in and most sections will be left empty. Start with the
					minimum viable structure and add sections only when you repeatedly find
					yourself needing them. Five good sections beat fifteen empty ones.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
